🛡️ S.H.I.E.L.D. Biometric Scanner — System Documentation
This web application performs real-time facial detection and recognition directly inside the user's web browser, featuring a Marvel/S.H.I.E.L.D.-inspired HUD theme.

🛠️ Key Technologies Used
HTML5 & CSS3: Formats the Marvel-inspired grid HUD, camera viewport, laser scan animations, and glowing UI panels.

JavaScript (ES6+): Handles video rendering, state management, file parsing, and dynamic UI updates.

face-api.js (TensorFlow.js wrapper): A browser-based machine learning library used to detect face locations, extract 68-point facial landmarks, and calculate 128-vector facial descriptors.

Web Speech API (window.speechSynthesis): Provides realistic audio voice readouts (JARVIS-style alerts) when a target is enrolled or recognized.

HTML5 MediaDevices API (getUserMedia): Requests real-time access to the user's webcam feed.

🧠 What Information the App Has vs. What It Needs
1. Pre-loaded Models (What the App Downloads on Startup)
When the webpage loads, face-api.js downloads three lightweight neural network models from a CDN:

SSD MobileNet V1: Locates faces in an image/frame and returns bounding boxes.

68 Point Face Landmark Detection Model: Maps specific facial contours (eyes, nose, jawline, mouth).

Face Recognition Model: Converts facial landmarks into a numerical array of 128 numbers (a Face Descriptor).

2. User Input (What the App Needs from You)
Webcam Permission: Required to capture real-time video frames.

Target Reference Image: A standard picture file (.jpg, .png, etc.) containing a visible face.

Target Label: The text name corresponding to the person in the reference image.

⚙️ Step-by-Step Architecture & How It Was Built
Step 1: Request Camera Access & Load Neural Networks
The application initializes by fetching the AI neural network models asynchronously.

Once the models load, navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia() prompts the user for camera access.

The webcam stream is attached directly to the <video> element.

Step 2: Facial Enrollment & Descriptor Extraction
When you upload an image and input a name, the app parses the image file using faceapi.bufferToImage().

The AI model analyzes the image to detect the face and extracts its 128-point facial descriptor.

The descriptor is paired with the string name inside a LabeledFaceDescriptors object and stored in memory (labeledDescriptors array).

A FaceMatcher object is created with a distance threshold (similarity tolerance) set to 0.6.

Step 3: Real-Time Camera Detection Loop
An automated loop runs every 200 milliseconds (setInterval).

The model scans the current frame of the <video> element to detect any present faces.

The bounding box coordinates are scaled to match the video element's dimensions.

Step 4: Face Matching & Audio Visual Feedback
For each detected face in the camera frame, its live descriptor is compared against all enrolled target descriptors in memory using Euclidean distance calculation.

If a match is found within the threshold:

A neon blue bounding box is drawn over the canvas.

The name tag is displayed above the box.

The Web Speech API triggers a voice alert: "Target identified: [Name]".

If no match is found (or no targets are enrolled):

A red bounding box is drawn indicating an "Unidentified Subject".

📂 How Others Can Build This (Quick Summary for GitHub)
Set Up the Structure: Create a single HTML file containing a video player, an overlay <canvas>, an enrollment form, and styling elements.

Include face-api.js: Import the CDN script in the <head> tag.

Initialize the Models & Camera: Use async/await to load the SSD MobileNet, Landmark, and Recognition models before requesting getUserMedia.

Build the Enrollment Handler: Listen to the form submit event, extract face descriptors from uploaded images, and store them in an array.

Run the Detection Loop: Set up a continuous interval to inspect video frames, match face vectors against stored targets, render bounding boxes on the canvas, and play audio feedback.
